  "id": "DrugBank_DB02362 - MONDO_5100",
  "verbalization": "Aminobenzoic Acid appears to have a potential role in the pathogenesis of Systemic Sclerosis through a series of indirect pathways involving other compounds. The evidence suggests that Aminobenzoic Acid may act as a precursor or mediator in the development of Systemic Sclerosis, as it is linked to Dermatomyositis, Localized Scleroderma, and Balanitis Xerotica Obliterans, all of which are associated with the condition. These conditions are often linked to the overactivation of fibroblast proliferation and extracellular matrix deposition, mechanisms that are also implicated in Systemic Sclerosis. The presence of a shared pathway involving Benzoic Acid, which is indicated in multiple contexts, further supports the notion that Aminobenzoic Acid may influence systemic processes through a chain of interactions that ultimately contribute to the development of Systemic Sclerosis. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism where Aminobenzoic Acid may modulate inflammatory or fibrotic processes, though the exact nature of its interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
